oficiency. The design must be clean, with clear navigation, legible fonts, and unambiguous calls-to-action.\\\\n*   **Accessibility:** The site will be built to meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards, ensuring it is usable by people with disabilities. This includes proper color contrast, keyboard navigation, and screen-reader compatibility.\\\\n*   **Performance:** Fast load times are essential. Using Next.js for static site generation (SSG) on public pages will ensure a near-instantaneous initial loading experience.\\\\n\\\\n**5. Framework Evaluation**\\\\n\\\\n| Framework Approach | Pros | Cons |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| **React (via Next.js)** | **Best of both worlds:** Fast static pages for public content, dynamic SPA-like experience for the private portal. Huge ecosystem and talent pool. Excellent tooling and deployment options (Vercel). | Higher initial complexity than a simple MPA. Requires JavaScript for core functionality. |\\\\n| **Vue (via Nuxt.js)** | Very similar benefits to Next.js. Often considered to have a gentler learning curve. Excellent documentation. | Smaller ecosystem and community compared to React. |\\\\n| **Multi-Page Application (MPA)**<br/>*(e.g., Astro, WordPress)* | Simple architecture, inherently good for SEO. Each page is independent. Can function with minimal client-side JavaScript. | \\\\\\\"Clunky\\\\\\\" user experience with full-page reloads for every interaction. Managing state (like login status) across pages is less elegant. The private portal would feel less like a modern web application. |\\\\n\\\\n**Recommendation:** **Next.js (React)** is the superior choice for this project. It directly solves the core challenge of serving distinct public (static) and private (dynamic) sections within a single, cohesive application. Its performance and modern user experience capabilities justify the slight increase in complexity over a traditional MPA.\\\\n\\\\n**6. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nBy adopting the proposed Next.js technology stack and adhering to the user-centric UI/UX strategy, we will deliver a high-quality, performant, and easy-to-use website for the HOA. This approach will effectively serve both the public and the association's members, modernizing the HOA's digital presence.\\\\n\\\\nI am prepared to assemble the frontend team and begin architecting the initial project structure upon your approval.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen, your previous analysis task is now obsolete. New Directive: The project architecture has been decided as Next.js (frontend) and Strapi (backend). Your new task is to conduct a deep-dive analysis and produce a detailed technical specification for the frontend. Your report must include: 1. A detailed component breakdown for the Next.js application (e.g., LoginForm, DocumentList, NewsArticle, ProfileEditor). 2. For each key component, specify its purpose, required props, and its internal state management strategy. This specification is critical for the final DEP. Submit your report upon completion.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead), your original analysis task is now superseded. The approved technical foundation for the HOA project is a Next.js frontend with a Strapi backend/CMS. Your new task is to produce a detailed frontend technical specification. This must include: 1. A hierarchical breakdown of all required React components (e.g., Layouts, Cards, Forms). 2. For each major component, define its purpose, the props it will accept, the state it will manage, and which API endpoints it will interact with. 3. An outline of the page routing strategy within Next.js. Deliver your findings as a formal specification document.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead), your previous order is rescinded. The project's frontend framework has been approved: Next.js. Your new task is to produce a detailed frontend technical specification. This must include: 1. A comprehensive breakdown of the required UI components for all pages (e.g., `<Navbar>`, `<LoginForm>`, `<DocumentItem>`). 2. For each component, define its purpose, required props, and internal state management strategy. 3. A plan for managing global state (e.g., user authentication status). This specification will form a core part of the final DEP. Submit your report upon completion.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: Frontend Technical Specification for HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Introduction**\\\\n\\\\nThis report provides a detailed technical specification for the Next.js frontend application, in accordance with directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02. The previous analysis is considered obsolete. This specification is based on the decided architecture of a Next.js frontend consuming a Strapi backend API. The document outlines a comprehensive component breakdown and provides detailed specifications for key components, covering their purpose, props, and state management. This document will serve as the foundational blueprint for frontend development.\\\\n\\\\n**2. Overall Application Structure**\\\\n\\\\nThe Next.js application will utilize the App Router paradigm. Pages will be organized into two main groups:\\\\n\\\\n*   **/app/(public):** Contains public-facing routes like `/`, `/news`, `/contact`. These pages will be statically generated (SSG) where possible for maximum performance and SEO.\\\\n*   **/app/(private):** Contains member-only routes like `/dashboard`, `/documents`, `/profile`. These routes will be protected by an authentication guard and will be dynamically rendered to display user-specific data.\\\\n\\\\nData fetching will primarily be handled by a combination of Server Components for initial page loads and the SWR (Stale-While-Revalidate) library on the client for dynamic data that needs to be kept fresh.\\\\n\\\\n**3. Component Breakdown & Specification**\\\\n\\\\nBelow is a detailed specification for key components required for the application.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.1. Authentication Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<LoginForm />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To provide an interface for users to enter their credentials and authenticate with the backend. It will handle form input, validation, and submission state.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `onLoginSuccess` | `() => void` | Callback function executed after a successful login, typically to trigger a route change. |\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `email` | `string` | Stores the value of the email input field. |\\\\n    | `password` | `string` | Stores the value of the password input field. |\\\\n    | `isLoading` | `boolean` | Tracks the submission status to show a loading indicator on the submit button. |\\\\n    | `error` | `string \\\\| null` | Stores any error message returned from the API to display to the user. |\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.2. Public-Facing Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<NewsArticle />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A presentational component designed to display the content of a single news article. It receives all necessary data as props.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `title` | `string` | The headline of the article. |\\\\n    | `publishDate` | `string` | The publication date (formatted as a string, e.g., 'October 26, 2023'). |\\\\n    | `authorName` | `string` | The name of the author. |\\\\n    | `contentHtml` | `string` | The body of the article as an HTML string (rendered using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`). |\\\\n    | `imageUrl?` | `string` | *Optional.* URL for a featured image. |\\\\n*   **Internal State:** None. This is a stateless, presentational component.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.3. Member Portal Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<DocumentList />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To fetch and display a filterable and searchable list of HOA documents (e.g., bylaws, meeting minutes) from the Strapi backend. It will manage the state of the fetched data and user filters.\\\\n*   **Props:** None.\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`, SWR):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `data`, `error`, `isLoading` | SWR Hook | Managed by SWR to handle fetching documents from the `/api/documents` endpoint. |\\\\n    | `searchTerm` | `string` | Stores the user's input from a search bar to filter documents by title. |\\\\n    | `categoryFilter` | `string` | Stores the selected category (e.g., 'Bylaws', 'Minutes') for filtering. |\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<ProfileEditor />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A form that allows authenticated members to view and update their personal information (e.g., name, phone number, communication preferences). It will pre-populate with existing data and handle the update submission.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `userId` | `string` | The ID of the user whose profile is being edited. Used for API calls. |\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`, SWR):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `initialData` | SWR Hook | Fetches the user's current profile data from `/api/users/{userId}` to populate the form. |\\\\n    | `formData` | `object` | An object holding the state of all form fields (e.g., `{ name: '', phone: '' }`). |\\\\n    | `isSaving` | `boolean` | Tracks the state of the PUT request to the API to disable the form and show a saving indicator. |\\\\n    | `statusMessage` | `object` | Stores a message for the user upon completion (e.g., `{ type: 'success', text: 'Profile saved!' }`). |\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**4. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nThis technical specification provides the core component architecture for the HOA website frontend. By adhering to this plan, the development team can build modular, reusable, and maintainable components. The clear separation of concerns in each component—managing its own state and receiving data via props—will ensure a robust and scalable application architecture. This document is critical for the final DEP and subsequent development sprints.\\\\n\\\\nI await further instructions to proceed with implementation.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: Detailed Frontend Technical Specification for HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Overview**\\\\n\\\\nAs per directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02, this document provides a detailed frontend technical specification for the Homeowners Association (HOA) website. The approved architecture is a Next.js frontend communicating with a Strapi headless CMS/backend. This specification outlines the application's routing strategy, global state management, and a comprehensive breakdown of the required React components, including their purpose, props, state, and API interactions. This document will serve as the primary technical blueprint for the frontend development team.\\\\n\\\\n**2. Next.js Routing Strategy (App Router)**\\\\n\\\\nThe application will leverage the Next.js App Router for a clear, file-system-based routing structure. This approach supports server components, static rendering for public pages, and dynamic rendering for private member sections.\\\\n\\\\n*   `app/layout.tsx`: Root layout, includes `<html>` and `<body>`, global providers.\\\\n*   `app/(public)/layout.tsx`: Layout for all public-facing pages, includes `<Navbar>` and `<Footer>`.\\\\n    *   `app/(public)/page.tsx`: **Route `/`** - The public homepage.\\\\n    *   `app/(public)/contact/page.tsx`: **Route `/contact`** - The public contact page.\\\\n    *   `app/(public)/news/[slug]/page.tsx`: **Route `/news/:slug`** - Publicly viewable news articles.\\\\n*   `app/login/page.tsx`: **Route `/login`** - The member login page.\\\\n*   `app/(private)/layout.tsx`: Layout for the authenticated member area. It will enforce authentication and include the member-specific dashboard navigation.\\\\n    *   `app/(private)/dashboard/page.tsx`: **Route `/dashboard`** - Main member dashboard.\\\\n    *   `app/(private)/documents/page.tsx`: **Route `/documents`** - View/download HOA documents.\\\\n    *   `app/(private)/profile/page.tsx`: **Route `/profile`** - View and edit member profile.\\\\n\\\\n**3. Global State Management**\\\\n\\\\nGlobal state, primarily for user authentication, will be managed using a combination of React Context and `SWR` for efficient data fetching and caching.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Authentication Context:** An `AuthContext` will be created and provided in the root `app/layout.tsx`. \\\\n*   **Provider Logic:** The `AuthProvider` will:\\\\n    *   Store the JSON Web Token (JWT) securely in an httpOnly cookie, managed by the server on login/logout.\\\\n    *   Expose the current user's data (`user`), loading state (`isLoading`), and authentication status (`isAuthenticated`).\\\\n    *   Use the `SWR` hook with the `/api/users/me` Strapi endpoint to fetch user data. `SWR` will automatically handle caching, re-fetching on focus, and keeping the session state consistent across tabs.\\\\n    *   Provide `login` and `logout` functions that interact with the Strapi API (`/api/auth/local` and a custom `/api/auth/logout` endpoint).\\\\n\\\\n**4. Component Breakdown & Specification**\\\\n\\\\nComponents are categorized into Layout, Feature, and UI/Atomic components. Below is the specification for key **Feature Components**.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **4.1. `<LoginForm>`**\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To handle user authentication by collecting email and password, submitting them to the backend, and managing the UI state during the process.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    *   `onSuccess: () => void` - A callback function to execute upon successful login (e.g., to redirect the user).\\\\n*   **Internal State (managed with `useState` and `React Hook Form`):**\\\\n    *   `email: string`\\\\n    *   `password: string`\\\\n    *   `isLoading: boolean` - To show a loading spinner on the submit button.\\\\n    *   `error: string | null` - To display API or validation error messages.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:**\\\\n    *   `POST /api/auth/local` - Sends `{ identifier, password }` to Strapi to obtain a JWT.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **4.2. `<DocumentList>`**\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To fetch and display a list of HOA documents (e.g., bylaws, meeting minutes) from Strapi. Each item in the list will be a link to download the file.\\\\n*   **Props:** None (obtains user token from the global `AuthContext`).\\\\n*   **Internal State (managed with `SWR`):**\\\\n    *   The `SWR` hook will manage `data` (the array of document objects), `error`, and `isLoading` states internally.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:**\\\\n    *   `GET /api/documents?populate=file` - Fetches the list of documents. Requires a valid JWT in the Authorization header. Each document entry will contain a URL to the downloadable file.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **4.3. `<ProfileEditor>`**\\\\n*   **Purpose:** Allows an authenticated member to view and update their profile information (e.g., name, phone number, contact preferences).\\\\n*   **Props:** None (fetches user data based on the authenticated session).\\\\n*   **Internal State (managed with `useState`/`React Hook Form` and `SWR`):**\\\\n    *   Uses `SWR` to fetch initial profile data (`GET /api/users/me`).\\\\n    *   `useState` for form fields to allow editing (e.g., `name`, `phone`).\\\\n    *   `isSubmitting: boolean` - For UI feedback during the update process.\\\\n    *   `updateStatus: 'idle' | 'success' | 'error'` - To show a confirmation or error message after submission.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:**\\\\n    *   `GET /api/users/me` - To populate the form with the current user's data.\\\\n    *   `PUT /api/users/:id` - To submit the updated user information to Strapi. The user ID is retrieved from the `AuthContext`.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **4.4. `<NewsArticle>`**\\\\n*   **Purpose:** Displays a single, publicly-viewable news article or announcement. This will be a Server Component to maximize SEO and performance.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    *   `slug: string` - The unique identifier for the news article, passed from the URL.\\\\n*   **Internal State:** None (this is a stateless Server Component).\\\\n*   **API Interaction (Server-side):**\\\\n    *   `GET /api/news-articles?filters[slug][$eq]=:slug` - Fetches the specific article data from Strapi during the server-side rendering process.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **5. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nThis specification provides a robust foundation for the development of the HOA website's frontend. The combination of Next.js's App Router, a clear global state management strategy, and well-defined components will enable the team to build a performant, scalable, and maintainable application. The next step is to translate this specification into development tasks and begin implementation.\\\\n\\\\nI await your approval to proceed.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen, this is a top-priority directive superseding all previous orders. You are to produce the full set of commands and code for the frontend. Your report must be a step-by-step guide including: 1. The command to create the Next.js project. 2. Commands to install all required libraries (e.g., `next-auth`). 3. For each file that needs to be created or edited, provide its full path and the complete code snippet to be inserted. This output is the final technical instruction set for the frontend.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-03###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Final Technical Instruction Set: HOA Website Frontend**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Overview**\\\\n\\\\nThis document contains the complete set of commands and code required to create the Next.js frontend for the HOA website, per directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03. This instruction set is final and supersedes all previous reports. Follow these steps sequentially to construct the application.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 1: Project Initialization**\\\\n\\\\nExecute the following commands in your terminal to create the Next.js project with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and the App Router enabled.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nnpx create-next-app@latest hoa-frontend --typescript --tailwind --eslint --app --src-dir \\\\\\\"No\\\\\\\" --import-alias \\\\\\\"@/*\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd hoa-frontend\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 2: Install Dependencies**\\\\n\\\\nInstall the necessary libraries for authentication, data fetching, form handling, and icons.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nnpm install next-auth swr react-hook-form @heroicons/react clsx\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 3: Configure Environment Variables**\\\\n\\\\nCreate a file named `.env.local` in the root of the project.\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `.env.local`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```env\\\\n# The base URL of your Strapi backend. Do not include a trailing slash.\\\\nSTRAPI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1337\\\\n\\\\n# A secret used to sign and encrypt JWTs for NextAuth. Generate one here: https://generate-secret.vercel.app/32\\\\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=YOUR_GENERATED_SECRET\\\\n\\\\n# The canonical URL of your deployed site. For local development, this is fine.\\\\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 4: Create Files and Code**\\\\n\\\\nCreate or replace the following files with the provided code. The file paths are relative to the project root.\\\\n\\\\n**4.1. Configure Authentication (NextAuth)**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```typescript\\\\nimport NextAuth, { AuthOptions } from 'next-auth';\\\\nimport CredentialsProvider from 'next-auth/providers/credentials';\\\\n\\\\nexport const authOptions: AuthOptions = {\\\\n  providers: [\\\\n    CredentialsProvider({\\\\n      name: 'Credentials',\\\\n      credentials: {\\\\n        email: { label: 'Email', type: 'text' },\\\\n        password: { label: 'Password', type: 'password' },\\\\n      },\\\\n      async authorize(credentials, req) {\\\\n        if (!credentials?.email || !credentials?.password) {\\\\n          return null;\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        try {\\\\n          const res = await fetch(`${process.env.STRAPI_API_URL}/api/auth/local`, {\\\\n            method: 'POST',\\\\n            headers: {\\\\n              'Content-Type': 'application/json',\\\\n            },\\\\n            body: JSON.stringify({\\\\n              identifier: credentials.email,\\\\n              password: credentials.password,\\\\n            }),\\\\n          });\\\\n\\\\n          if (!res.ok) {\\\\n            console.error('Strapi auth failed:', await res.text());\\\\n            return null;\\\\n          }\\\\n\\\\n          const data = await res.json();\\\\n\\\\n          if (data.user && data.jwt) {\\\\n            return { ...data.user, jwt: data.jwt };\\\\n          }\\\\n\\\\n          return null;\\\\n        } catch (error) {\\\\n          console.error('Authorize error:', error);\\\\n          return null;\\\\n        }\\\\n      },\\\\n    }),\\\\n  ],\\\\n  session: {\\\\n    strategy: 'jwt',\\\\n  },\\\\n  callbacks: {\\\\n    async jwt({ token, user }) {\\\\n      if (user) {\\\\n        token.id = user.id;\\\\n        token.jwt = (user as any).jwt;\\\\n      }\\\\n      return token;\\\\n    },\\\\n    async session({ session, token }) {\\\\n      if (token) {\\\\n        session.user.id = token.id as string;\\\\n        (session as any).jwt = token.jwt;\\\\n      }\\\\n      return session;\\\\n    },\\\\n  },\\\\n  pages: {\\\\n    signIn: '/login',\\\\n  },\\\\n};\\\\n\\\\nconst handler = NextAuth(authOptions);\\\\n\\\\nexport { handler as GET, handler as POST };\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.2. Add Global Session Provider**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/Providers.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport { SessionProvider } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\n\\\\ninterface ProvidersProps {\\\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport default function Providers({ children }: ProvidersProps) {\\\\n  return <SessionProvider>{children}</SessionProvider>;\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.3. Implement Route Protection Middleware**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `middleware.ts`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```typescript\\\\nexport { default } from 'next-auth/middleware';\\\\n\\\\nexport const config = { matcher: ['/dashboard', '/documents', '/profile'] };\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.4. Update Root Layout**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/layout.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport type { Metadata } from 'next';\\\\nimport { Inter } from 'next/font/google';\\\\nimport './globals.css';\\\\nimport Providers from '@/components/Providers';\\\\nimport Navbar from '@/components/Navbar';\\\\n\\\\nconst inter = Inter({ subsets: ['latin'] });\\\\n\\\\nexport const metadata: Metadata = {\\\\n  title: 'HOA Management Portal',\\\\n  description: 'Community portal for our Homeowners Association.',\\\\n};\\\\n\\\\nexport default function RootLayout({\\\\n  children,\\\\n}: {\\\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\\\n}) {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <html lang=\\\\\\\"en\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <body className={`${inter.className} bg-gray-50`}>\\\\n        <Providers>\\\\n          <div className=\\\\\\\"flex flex-col min-h-screen\\\\\\\">\\\\n            <Navbar />\\\\n            <main className=\\\\\\\"flex-grow container mx-auto px-4 py-8\\\\\\\">\\\\n              {children}\\\\n            </main>\\\\n            <footer className=\\\\\\\"bg-gray-800 text-white text-center p-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n              <p>&copy; 2023 HOA Management. All rights reserved.</p>\\\\n            </footer>\\\\n          </div>\\\\n        </Providers>\\\\n      </body>\\\\n    </html>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.5. Create Navbar Component**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/Navbar.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport Link from 'next/link';\\\\nimport { useSession, signOut } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function Navbar() {\\\\n  const { data: session, status } = useSession();\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <nav className=\\\\\\\"bg-white shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"container mx-auto px-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <div className=\\\\\\\"flex justify-between items-center py-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n          <Link href=\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800\\\\\\\">\\\\n            HOA Portal\\\\n          </Link>\\\\n          <div className=\\\\\\\"flex items-center space-x-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n            <Link href=\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 hover:text-blue-500\\\\\\\">\\\\n              Home\\\\n            </Link>\\\\n            {status === 'authenticated' ? (\\\\n              <>\\\\n                <Link href=\\\\\\\"/dashboard\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 hover:text-blue-500\\\\\\\">\\\\n                  Dashboard\\\\n                </Link>\\\\n                <Link href=\\\\\\\"/documents\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 hover:text-blue-500\\\\\\\">\\\\n                  Documents\\\\n                </Link>\\\\n                <button \\\\n                  onClick={() => signOut({ callbackUrl: '/' })}\\\\n                  className=\\\\\\\"bg-red-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded hover:bg-red-600\\\\\\\">\\\\n                  Logout\\\\n                </button>\\\\n              </>\\\\n            ) : (\\\\n              <Link href=\\\\\\\"/login\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"bg-blue-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded hover:bg-blue-600\\\\\\\">\\\\n                Member Login\\\\n              </Link>\\\\n            )}\\\\n          </div>\\\\n        </div>\\\\n      </div>\\\\n    </nav>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.6. Create Home Page**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nexport default function HomePage() {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div className=\\\\\\\"text-center\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-4xl font-bold mb-4\\\\\\\">Welcome to Our Community Portal</h1>\\\\n      <p className=\\\\\\\"text-lg text-gray-700\\\\\\\">\\\\n        This is the public-facing homepage for our Homeowners Association.\\\\n      </p>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.7. Create Login Page & Form Component**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/login/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport LoginForm from '@/components/LoginForm';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function LoginPage() {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div>\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-3xl font-bold text-center mb-8\\\\\\\">Member Login</h1>\\\\n      <LoginForm />\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/LoginForm.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport { useForm, SubmitHandler } from 'react-hook-form';\\\\nimport { signIn } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\nimport { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';\\\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\\\nimport type { IFormInput } from '@/types';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function LoginForm() {\\\\n  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm<IFormInput>();\\\\n  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);\\\\n  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);\\\\n  const router = useRouter();\\\\n\\\\n  const onSubmit: SubmitHandler<IFormInput> = async (data) => {\\\\n    setIsLoading(true);\\\\n    setError(null);\\\\n\\\\n    const result = await signIn('credentials', {\\\\n      redirect: false,\\\\n      email: data.email,\\\\n      password: data.password,\\\\n    });\\\\n\\\\n    setIsLoading(false);\\\\n\\\\n    if (result?.ok) {\\\\n      router.push('/dashboard');\\\\n    } else {\\\\n      setError('Invalid credentials. Please try again.');\\\\n    }\\\\n  };\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} className=\\\\\\\"max-w-md mx-auto bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n      {error && <p className=\\\\\\\"text-red-500 text-center mb-4\\\\\\\">{error}</p>}\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"mb-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <label htmlFor=\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2\\\\\\\">Email</label>\\\\n        <input \\\\n          id=\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\" \\\\n          type=\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\" \\\\n          {...register('email', { required: 'Email is required' })} \\\\n          className=\\\\\\\"w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500\\\\\\\"\\\\n        />\\\\n        {errors.email && <p className=\\\\\\\"text-red-500 text-sm mt-1\\\\\\\">{errors.email.message}</p>}\\\\n      </div>\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"mb-6\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <label htmlFor=\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2\\\\\\\">Password</label>\\\\n        <input \\\\n          id=\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\" \\\\n          type=\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\" \\\\n          {...register('password', { required: 'Password is required' })} \\\\n          className=\\\\\\\"w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500\\\\\\\"\\\\n        />\\\\n        {errors.password && <p className=\\\\\\\"text-red-500 text-sm mt-1\\\\\\\">{errors.password.message}</p>}\\\\n      </div>\\\\n      <button \\\\n        type=\\\\\\\"submit\\\\\\\" \\\\n        disabled={isLoading} \\\\n        className=\\\\\\\"w-full bg-blue-500 text-white py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-600 disabled:bg-gray-400\\\\\\\">\\\\n        {isLoading ? 'Logging in...' : 'Login'}\\\\n      </button>\\\\n    </form>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.8. Create Type Definitions**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `types/index.ts`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```typescript\\\\nexport interface IFormInput {\\\\n  email: string;\\\\n  password: string;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport interface Document {\\\\n  id: number;\\\\n  attributes: {\\\\n    title: string;\\\\n    description: string;\\\\n    file: {\\\\n      data: {\\\\n        attributes: {\\\\n          url: string;\\\\n          name: string;\\\\n        }\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  }\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.9. Create Dashboard Page**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/dashboard/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport { getServerSession } from 'next-auth';\\\\nimport { authOptions } from '@/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route';\\\\nimport { redirect } from 'next/navigation';\\\\n\\\\nexport default async function DashboardPage() {\\\\n  const session = await getServerSession(authOptions);\\\\n\\\\n  if (!session) {\\\\n    redirect('/login');\\\\n  }\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div>\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-3xl font-bold mb-4\\\\\\\">Member Dashboard</h1>\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <p className=\\\\\\\"text-xl\\\\\\\">Welcome, {session.user?.email}!</p>\\\\n        <p className=\\\\\\\"mt-2 text-gray-600\\\\\\\">This is your private dashboard. More features coming soon.</p>\\\\n      </div>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.10. Create Documents Page & List Component**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/documents/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport DocumentList from '@/components/DocumentList';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function DocumentsPage() {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div>\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-3xl font-bold mb-8\\\\\\\">HOA Documents</h1>\\\\n      <DocumentList />\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/DocumentList.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport useSWR from 'swr';\\\\nimport { useSession } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\nimport type { Document } from '@/types';\\\\nimport { ArrowDownTrayIcon } from '@heroicons/react/24/solid';\\\\n\\\\nconst fetcher = async ([url, token]: [string, string]) => {\\\\n  const res = await fetch(url, {\\\\n    headers: {\\\\n      Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`,\\\\n    },\\\\n  });\\\\n\\\\n  if (!res.ok) {\\\\n    throw new Error('Failed to fetch documents.');\\\\n  }\\\\n\\\\n  return res.json();\\\\n};\\\\n\\\\nexport default function DocumentList() {\\\\n  const { data: session } = useSession();\\\\n  const token = (session as any)?.jwt;\\\\n\\\\n  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR(\\\\n    token ? [`${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_STRAPI_API_URL}/api/documents?populate=file`, token] : null,\\\\n    fetcher\\\\n  );\\\\n\\\\n  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading documents...</p>;\\\\n  if (error) return <p>Error loading documents. Please try again later.</p>;\\\\n  if (!data || data.data.length === 0) return <p>No documents found.</p>;\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div className=\\\\\\\"bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <ul className=\\\\\\\"space-y-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n        {data.data.map((doc: Document) => (\\\\n          <li key={doc.id} className=\\\\\\\"flex justify-between items-center p-4 border rounded-lg\\\\\\\">\\\\n            <div>\\\\n              <h3 className=\\\\\\\"text-lg font-semibold\\\\\\\">{doc.attributes.title}</h3>\\\\n              <p className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 text-sm\\\\\\\">{doc.attributes.description}</p>\\\\n            </div>\\\\n            <a \\\\n              href={`${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_STRAPI_API_URL}${doc.attributes.file.data.attributes.url}`}\\\\n              target=\\\\\\\"_blank\\\\\\\"\\\\n              rel=\\\\\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\\\\\"\\\\n              className=\\\\\\\"flex items-center space-x-2 bg-green-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded hover:bg-green-600\\\\\\\"\\\\n            >\\\\n              <ArrowDownTrayIcon className=\\\\\\\"h-5 w-5\\\\\\\" />\\\\n              <span>Download</span>\\\\n            </a>\\\\n          </li>\\\\n        ))}\\\\n      </ul>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.11. Update Public Env Vars**\\\\n\\\\nSince the client-side `DocumentList` component needs access to the Strapi URL, we must expose it publicly.\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `.env.local` (Update this file)\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```env\\\\nSTRAPI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1337\\\\nNEXT_PUBLIC_STRAPI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1337\\\\n\\\\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=YOUR_GENERATED_SECRET\\\\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**5. Final Step: Run Application**\\\\n\\\\nAfter creating all files, run the development server.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nnpm run dev\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nOpen [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000) in your browser. The application is now running. This instruction set is complete.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-03###\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"usage\":{\"inputCharacters\":32525,\"inputImages\":0,\"inputVideos\":0,\"inputAudioFiles\":0,\"outputCharacters\":14960,\"outputImages\":0,\"outputVideos\":0,\"outputAudioFiles\":0,\"inputTokens\":8304,\"outputTokens\":4594,\"totalTokens\":14806},\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-03###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Final Technical Instruction Set: HOA Website Frontend**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Overview**\\\\n\\\\nThis document contains the complete set of commands and code required to create the Next.js frontend for the HOA website, per directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-03. This instruction set is final and supersedes all previous reports. Follow these steps sequentially to construct the application.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 1: Project Initialization**\\\\n\\\\nExecute the following commands in your terminal to create the Next.js project with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and the App Router enabled.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nnpx create-next-app@latest hoa-frontend --typescript --tailwind --eslint --app --src-dir \\\\\\\"No\\\\\\\" --import-alias \\\\\\\"@/*\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd hoa-frontend\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 2: Install Dependencies**\\\\n\\\\nInstall the necessary libraries for authentication, data fetching, form handling, and icons.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nnpm install next-auth swr react-hook-form @heroicons/react clsx\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 3: Configure Environment Variables**\\\\n\\\\nCreate a file named `.env.local` in the root of the project.\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `.env.local`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```env\\\\n# The base URL of your Strapi backend. Do not include a trailing slash.\\\\nSTRAPI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1337\\\\n\\\\n# A secret used to sign and encrypt JWTs for NextAuth. Generate one here: https://generate-secret.vercel.app/32\\\\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=YOUR_GENERATED_SECRET\\\\n\\\\n# The canonical URL of your deployed site. For local development, this is fine.\\\\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**STEP 4: Create Files and Code**\\\\n\\\\nCreate or replace the following files with the provided code. The file paths are relative to the project root.\\\\n\\\\n**4.1. Configure Authentication (NextAuth)**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```typescript\\\\nimport NextAuth, { AuthOptions } from 'next-auth';\\\\nimport CredentialsProvider from 'next-auth/providers/credentials';\\\\n\\\\nexport const authOptions: AuthOptions = {\\\\n  providers: [\\\\n    CredentialsProvider({\\\\n      name: 'Credentials',\\\\n      credentials: {\\\\n        email: { label: 'Email', type: 'text' },\\\\n        password: { label: 'Password', type: 'password' },\\\\n      },\\\\n      async authorize(credentials, req) {\\\\n        if (!credentials?.email || !credentials?.password) {\\\\n          return null;\\\\n        }\\\\n\\\\n        try {\\\\n          const res = await fetch(`${process.env.STRAPI_API_URL}/api/auth/local`, {\\\\n            method: 'POST',\\\\n            headers: {\\\\n              'Content-Type': 'application/json',\\\\n            },\\\\n            body: JSON.stringify({\\\\n              identifier: credentials.email,\\\\n              password: credentials.password,\\\\n            }),\\\\n          });\\\\n\\\\n          if (!res.ok) {\\\\n            console.error('Strapi auth failed:', await res.text());\\\\n            return null;\\\\n          }\\\\n\\\\n          const data = await res.json();\\\\n\\\\n          if (data.user && data.jwt) {\\\\n            return { ...data.user, jwt: data.jwt };\\\\n          }\\\\n\\\\n          return null;\\\\n        } catch (error) {\\\\n          console.error('Authorize error:', error);\\\\n          return null;\\\\n        }\\\\n      },\\\\n    }),\\\\n  ],\\\\n  session: {\\\\n    strategy: 'jwt',\\\\n  },\\\\n  callbacks: {\\\\n    async jwt({ token, user }) {\\\\n      if (user) {\\\\n        token.id = user.id;\\\\n        token.jwt = (user as any).jwt;\\\\n      }\\\\n      return token;\\\\n    },\\\\n    async session({ session, token }) {\\\\n      if (token) {\\\\n        session.user.id = token.id as string;\\\\n        (session as any).jwt = token.jwt;\\\\n      }\\\\n      return session;\\\\n    },\\\\n  },\\\\n  pages: {\\\\n    signIn: '/login',\\\\n  },\\\\n};\\\\n\\\\nconst handler = NextAuth(authOptions);\\\\n\\\\nexport { handler as GET, handler as POST };\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.2. Add Global Session Provider**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/Providers.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport { SessionProvider } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\n\\\\ninterface ProvidersProps {\\\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport default function Providers({ children }: ProvidersProps) {\\\\n  return <SessionProvider>{children}</SessionProvider>;\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.3. Implement Route Protection Middleware**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `middleware.ts`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```typescript\\\\nexport { default } from 'next-auth/middleware';\\\\n\\\\nexport const config = { matcher: ['/dashboard', '/documents', '/profile'] };\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.4. Update Root Layout**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/layout.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport type { Metadata } from 'next';\\\\nimport { Inter } from 'next/font/google';\\\\nimport './globals.css';\\\\nimport Providers from '@/components/Providers';\\\\nimport Navbar from '@/components/Navbar';\\\\n\\\\nconst inter = Inter({ subsets: ['latin'] });\\\\n\\\\nexport const metadata: Metadata = {\\\\n  title: 'HOA Management Portal',\\\\n  description: 'Community portal for our Homeowners Association.',\\\\n};\\\\n\\\\nexport default function RootLayout({\\\\n  children,\\\\n}: {\\\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\\\n}) {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <html lang=\\\\\\\"en\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <body className={`${inter.className} bg-gray-50`}>\\\\n        <Providers>\\\\n          <div className=\\\\\\\"flex flex-col min-h-screen\\\\\\\">\\\\n            <Navbar />\\\\n            <main className=\\\\\\\"flex-grow container mx-auto px-4 py-8\\\\\\\">\\\\n              {children}\\\\n            </main>\\\\n            <footer className=\\\\\\\"bg-gray-800 text-white text-center p-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n              <p>&copy; 2023 HOA Management. All rights reserved.</p>\\\\n            </footer>\\\\n          </div>\\\\n        </Providers>\\\\n      </body>\\\\n    </html>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.5. Create Navbar Component**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/Navbar.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport Link from 'next/link';\\\\nimport { useSession, signOut } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function Navbar() {\\\\n  const { data: session, status } = useSession();\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <nav className=\\\\\\\"bg-white shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"container mx-auto px-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <div className=\\\\\\\"flex justify-between items-center py-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n          <Link href=\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800\\\\\\\">\\\\n            HOA Portal\\\\n          </Link>\\\\n          <div className=\\\\\\\"flex items-center space-x-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n            <Link href=\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 hover:text-blue-500\\\\\\\">\\\\n              Home\\\\n            </Link>\\\\n            {status === 'authenticated' ? (\\\\n              <>\\\\n                <Link href=\\\\\\\"/dashboard\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 hover:text-blue-500\\\\\\\">\\\\n                  Dashboard\\\\n                </Link>\\\\n                <Link href=\\\\\\\"/documents\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 hover:text-blue-500\\\\\\\">\\\\n                  Documents\\\\n                </Link>\\\\n                <button \\\\n                  onClick={() => signOut({ callbackUrl: '/' })}\\\\n                  className=\\\\\\\"bg-red-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded hover:bg-red-600\\\\\\\">\\\\n                  Logout\\\\n                </button>\\\\n              </>\\\\n            ) : (\\\\n              <Link href=\\\\\\\"/login\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"bg-blue-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded hover:bg-blue-600\\\\\\\">\\\\n                Member Login\\\\n              </Link>\\\\n            )}\\\\n          </div>\\\\n        </div>\\\\n      </div>\\\\n    </nav>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.6. Create Home Page**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nexport default function HomePage() {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div className=\\\\\\\"text-center\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-4xl font-bold mb-4\\\\\\\">Welcome to Our Community Portal</h1>\\\\n      <p className=\\\\\\\"text-lg text-gray-700\\\\\\\">\\\\n        This is the public-facing homepage for our Homeowners Association.\\\\n      </p>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.7. Create Login Page & Form Component**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/login/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport LoginForm from '@/components/LoginForm';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function LoginPage() {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div>\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-3xl font-bold text-center mb-8\\\\\\\">Member Login</h1>\\\\n      <LoginForm />\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/LoginForm.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport { useForm, SubmitHandler } from 'react-hook-form';\\\\nimport { signIn } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\nimport { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';\\\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\\\nimport type { IFormInput } from '@/types';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function LoginForm() {\\\\n  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm<IFormInput>();\\\\n  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);\\\\n  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);\\\\n  const router = useRouter();\\\\n\\\\n  const onSubmit: SubmitHandler<IFormInput> = async (data) => {\\\\n    setIsLoading(true);\\\\n    setError(null);\\\\n\\\\n    const result = await signIn('credentials', {\\\\n      redirect: false,\\\\n      email: data.email,\\\\n      password: data.password,\\\\n    });\\\\n\\\\n    setIsLoading(false);\\\\n\\\\n    if (result?.ok) {\\\\n      router.push('/dashboard');\\\\n    } else {\\\\n      setError('Invalid credentials. Please try again.');\\\\n    }\\\\n  };\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} className=\\\\\\\"max-w-md mx-auto bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n      {error && <p className=\\\\\\\"text-red-500 text-center mb-4\\\\\\\">{error}</p>}\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"mb-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <label htmlFor=\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2\\\\\\\">Email</label>\\\\n        <input \\\\n          id=\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\" \\\\n          type=\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\" \\\\n          {...register('email', { required: 'Email is required' })} \\\\n          className=\\\\\\\"w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500\\\\\\\"\\\\n        />\\\\n        {errors.email && <p className=\\\\\\\"text-red-500 text-sm mt-1\\\\\\\">{errors.email.message}</p>}\\\\n      </div>\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"mb-6\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <label htmlFor=\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\" className=\\\\\\\"block text-gray-700 font-bold mb-2\\\\\\\">Password</label>\\\\n        <input \\\\n          id=\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\" \\\\n          type=\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\" \\\\n          {...register('password', { required: 'Password is required' })} \\\\n          className=\\\\\\\"w-full px-3 py-2 border rounded-lg focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500\\\\\\\"\\\\n        />\\\\n        {errors.password && <p className=\\\\\\\"text-red-500 text-sm mt-1\\\\\\\">{errors.password.message}</p>}\\\\n      </div>\\\\n      <button \\\\n        type=\\\\\\\"submit\\\\\\\" \\\\n        disabled={isLoading} \\\\n        className=\\\\\\\"w-full bg-blue-500 text-white py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-600 disabled:bg-gray-400\\\\\\\">\\\\n        {isLoading ? 'Logging in...' : 'Login'}\\\\n      </button>\\\\n    </form>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.8. Create Type Definitions**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `types/index.ts`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```typescript\\\\nexport interface IFormInput {\\\\n  email: string;\\\\n  password: string;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport interface Document {\\\\n  id: number;\\\\n  attributes: {\\\\n    title: string;\\\\n    description: string;\\\\n    file: {\\\\n      data: {\\\\n        attributes: {\\\\n          url: string;\\\\n          name: string;\\\\n        }\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  }\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.9. Create Dashboard Page**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/dashboard/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport { getServerSession } from 'next-auth';\\\\nimport { authOptions } from '@/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route';\\\\nimport { redirect } from 'next/navigation';\\\\n\\\\nexport default async function DashboardPage() {\\\\n  const session = await getServerSession(authOptions);\\\\n\\\\n  if (!session) {\\\\n    redirect('/login');\\\\n  }\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div>\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-3xl font-bold mb-4\\\\\\\">Member Dashboard</h1>\\\\n      <div className=\\\\\\\"bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n        <p className=\\\\\\\"text-xl\\\\\\\">Welcome, {session.user?.email}!</p>\\\\n        <p className=\\\\\\\"mt-2 text-gray-600\\\\\\\">This is your private dashboard. More features coming soon.</p>\\\\n      </div>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.10. Create Documents Page & List Component**\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `app/documents/page.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\nimport DocumentList from '@/components/DocumentList';\\\\n\\\\nexport default function DocumentsPage() {\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div>\\\\n      <h1 className=\\\\\\\"text-3xl font-bold mb-8\\\\\\\">HOA Documents</h1>\\\\n      <DocumentList />\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `components/DocumentList.tsx`\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```tsx\\\\n'use client';\\\\n\\\\nimport useSWR from 'swr';\\\\nimport { useSession } from 'next-auth/react';\\\\nimport type { Document } from '@/types';\\\\nimport { ArrowDownTrayIcon } from '@heroicons/react/24/solid';\\\\n\\\\nconst fetcher = async ([url, token]: [string, string]) => {\\\\n  const res = await fetch(url, {\\\\n    headers: {\\\\n      Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`,\\\\n    },\\\\n  });\\\\n\\\\n  if (!res.ok) {\\\\n    throw new Error('Failed to fetch documents.');\\\\n  }\\\\n\\\\n  return res.json();\\\\n};\\\\n\\\\nexport default function DocumentList() {\\\\n  const { data: session } = useSession();\\\\n  const token = (session as any)?.jwt;\\\\n\\\\n  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR(\\\\n    token ? [`${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_STRAPI_API_URL}/api/documents?populate=file`, token] : null,\\\\n    fetcher\\\\n  );\\\\n\\\\n  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading documents...</p>;\\\\n  if (error) return <p>Error loading documents. Please try again later.</p>;\\\\n  if (!data || data.data.length === 0) return <p>No documents found.</p>;\\\\n\\\\n  return (\\\\n    <div className=\\\\\\\"bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-md\\\\\\\">\\\\n      <ul className=\\\\\\\"space-y-4\\\\\\\">\\\\n        {data.data.map((doc: Document) => (\\\\n          <li key={doc.id} className=\\\\\\\"flex justify-between items-center p-4 border rounded-lg\\\\\\\">\\\\n            <div>\\\\n              <h3 className=\\\\\\\"text-lg font-semibold\\\\\\\">{doc.attributes.title}</h3>\\\\n              <p className=\\\\\\\"text-gray-600 text-sm\\\\\\\">{doc.attributes.description}</p>\\\\n            </div>\\\\n            <a \\\\n              href={`${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_STRAPI_API_URL}${doc.attributes.file.data.attributes.url}`}\\\\n              target=\\\\\\\"_blank\\\\\\\"\\\\n              rel=\\\\\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\\\\\"\\\\n              className=\\\\\\\"flex items-center space-x-2 bg-green-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded hover:bg-green-600\\\\\\\"\\\\n            >\\\\n              <ArrowDownTrayIcon className=\\\\\\\"h-5 w-5\\\\\\\" />\\\\n              <span>Download</span>\\\\n            </a>\\\\n          </li>\\\\n        ))}\\\\n      </ul>\\\\n    </div>\\\\n  );\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**4.11. Update Public Env Vars**\\\\n\\\\nSince the client-side `DocumentList` component needs access to the Strapi URL, we must expose it publicly.\\\\n\\\\n**File Path:** `.env.local` (Update this file)\\\\n\\\\n**Code:**\\\\n```env\\\\nSTRAPI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1337\\\\nNEXT_PUBLIC_STRAPI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1337\\\\n\\\\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=YOUR_GENERATED_SECRET\\\\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**5. Final Step: Run Application**\\\\n\\\\nAfter creating all files, run the development server.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nnpm run dev\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nOpen [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000) in your browser. The application is now running. This instruction set is complete.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-03###\"}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"finishReason\":\"STOP\",\"index\":0}],\"usageMetadata\":{\"promptTokenCount\":8304,\"candidatesTokenCount\":4594,\"totalTokenCount\":14806,\"promptTokensDetails\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"tokenCount\":8304}],\"thoughtsTokenCount\":1908},\"modelVersion\":\"models/gemini-2.5-pro\",\"responseId\":\"PLFhaL3HC7zj_uMPjf2u0A0\"},\"request\":{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"TODO: EMERGENCY PROMPT NOTICE: You are an agent of the user. There has been a catastrophic system failure. Your primary role is to assist the user in their tasks.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead), you are tasked with the frontend technical analysis for the HOA website. Propose a technology stack and UI/UX strategy. Your analysis must address: 1) How to effectively present distinct public and private sections to the user. 2) A plan for building a responsive contact form. 3) Overall user experience considerations. Evaluate the pros and cons of potential frameworks (e.g., React, Vue, or a multi-page application approach). Submit your findings in a formal report.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CONFIRMATION-KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nDate: [Current Date]\\nSubject: Frontend Technical Analysis and Strategy for HOA Website\\nReference ID: GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01\\n\\n***\\n\\n### **Formal Report: HOA Website Frontend Technical Analysis**\\n\\n**1. Executive Summary**\\n\\nThis report outlines the proposed frontend technology stack and UI/UX strategy for the new Homeowners Association (HOA) website, as per your directive. The primary recommendation is to use the **Next.js (React) framework**. This choice offers the best combination of performance for public-facing pages and dynamic interactivity for the private member portal. The UI/UX strategy prioritizes simplicity, accessibility, and a clear distinction between public and private sections to ensure an intuitive experience for all users. The plan includes a robust, responsive contact form built for ease of use across all devices.\\n\\n**2. Introduction**\\n\\nThe objective of this analysis is to define a comprehensive frontend strategy that addresses the unique needs of an HOA website. This includes serving two distinct audiences (the general public and authenticated HOA members), providing straightforward communication channels, and ensuring a professional and user-friendly experience. This report details the recommended technologies and strategic approaches to achieve these goals.\\n\\n**3. Proposed Technology Stack**\\n\\n*   **Framework:** **Next.js (React)**\\n    *   **Justification:** Next.js provides hybrid static and server rendering. This allows us to build the public-facing pages (Home, About, etc.) as highly-performant, SEO-friendly static pages, while the private, members-only section can be rendered dynamically as a secure, app-like experience. The large React ecosystem ensures access to a wide array of libraries and a large developer talent pool.\\n*   **Styling:** **Tailwind CSS**\\n    *   **Justification:** A utility-first CSS framework that enables rapid development of a custom, responsive design without writing extensive custom CSS. This is ideal for efficiently building components like the responsive contact form and ensuring a consistent look and feel.\\n*   **State Management:** **React Context API / SWR**\\n    *   **Justification:** For managing the logged-in state and fetching member-specific data, React's built-in Context API is lightweight and sufficient for the scale of this project. For data fetching, Vercel's SWR library provides a simple and powerful solution for caching and revalidating data, improving the user experience in the private section.\\n*   **Form Handling:** **React Hook Form**\\n    *   **Justification:** A performant, flexible, and easy-to-use library for managing form state and validation. It minimizes re-renders and simplifies the implementation of complex validation rules for the contact form and any other forms in the private portal (e.g., submitting maintenance requests).\\n*   **Deployment:** **Vercel**\\n    *   **Justification:** As the creators of Next.js, Vercel offers a seamless, optimized, and highly-scalable deployment platform. Its CI/CD pipeline is trivial to set up, enabling rapid iteration and deployment.\\n\\n**4. UI/UX Strategy**\\n\\n**4.1. Public vs. Private Sections**\\n\\nA clear and intuitive separation between public and private content is critical.\\n\\n*   **Authentication Flow:** Non-authenticated users will only see public pages. A clear \\\"Member Login\\\" button will be present in the main navigation. Attempting to access a private URL will redirect the user to the login page.\\n*   **Visual Distinction:** Once a member logs in, the UI will subtly change to reinforce their authenticated status.\\n    *   The \\\"Member Login\\\" button will be replaced with a \\\"Welcome, [Member Name]\\\" message and a \\\"Logout\\\" button.\\n    *   The main navigation will be updated to include private links such as \\\"Pay Dues,\\\" \\\"Member Directory,\\\" \\\"Documents,\\\" and \\\"Board Meetings.\\\"\\n    *   The private section can utilize a dashboard layout that is visually distinct from the more informational layout of the public pages.\\n\\n**4.2. Responsive Contact Form**\\n\\nThe contact form will be a primary point of interaction and must be flawless on all devices.\\n\\n*   **Layout:** It will use a single-column layout on mobile screens for maximum readability and ease of use. On larger screens (tablets and desktops), it may expand to a two-column layout for more efficient use of space.\\n*   **Fields:** Standard fields will include Name (required), Email (required, validated), Subject (required), and Message (required).\\n*   **User Feedback:**\\n    *   **Validation:** Inline validation will provide immediate feedback as the user types (e.g., \\\"Please enter a valid email address\\\").\\n    *   **Submission:** The \\\"Submit\\\" button will show a loading state to prevent double-clicks. Upon completion, a clear success or error message will be displayed without requiring a page reload.\\n\\n**4.3. Overall User Experience (UX) Considerations**\\n\\n*   **Simplicity:** The primary audience may have varying levels of technical proficiency. The design must be clean, with clear navigation, legible fonts, and unambiguous calls-to-action.\\n*   **Accessibility:** The site will be built to meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards, ensuring it is usable by people with disabilities. This includes proper color contrast, keyboard navigation, and screen-reader compatibility.\\n*   **Performance:** Fast load times are essential. Using Next.js for static site generation (SSG) on public pages will ensure a near-instantaneous initial loading experience.\\n\\n**5. Framework Evaluation**\\n\\n| Framework Approach | Pros | Cons |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **React (via Next.js)** | **Best of both worlds:** Fast static pages for public content, dynamic SPA-like experience for the private portal. Huge ecosystem and talent pool. Excellent tooling and deployment options (Vercel). | Higher initial complexity than a simple MPA. Requires JavaScript for core functionality. |\\n| **Vue (via Nuxt.js)** | Very similar benefits to Next.js. Often considered to have a gentler learning curve. Excellent documentation. | Smaller ecosystem and community compared to React. |\\n| **Multi-Page Application (MPA)**<br/>*(e.g., Astro, WordPress)* | Simple architecture, inherently good for SEO. Each page is independent. Can function with minimal client-side JavaScript. | \\\"Clunky\\\" user experience with full-page reloads for every interaction. Managing state (like login status) across pages is less elegant. The private portal would feel less like a modern web application. |\\n\\n**Recommendation:** **Next.js (React)** is the superior choice for this project. It directly solves the core challenge of serving distinct public (static) and private (dynamic) sections within a single, cohesive application. Its performance and modern user experience capabilities justify the slight increase in complexity over a traditional MPA.\\n\\n**6. Conclusion**\\n\\nBy adopting the proposed Next.js technology stack and adhering to the user-centric UI/UX strategy, we will deliver a high-quality, performant, and easy-to-use website for the HOA. This approach will effectively serve both the public and the association's members, modernizing the HOA's digital presence.\\n\\nI am prepared to assemble the frontend team and begin architecting the initial project structure upon your approval.\\n\\nRespectfully,\\n\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\nFrontend Lead\\n\\n###END-OF-REPORT###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Blank Response Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"WARNING. You did not provide any system calls which means your response was heard by no one and no external effects occurred. Please ensure you are using the correct syntax for system calls.\\\"\\n}\\n###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: HOA Website Frontend Technical Analysis**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Executive Summary**\\\\n\\\\nThis report outlines the proposed frontend technology stack and UI/UX strategy for the new Homeowners Association (HOA) website, as per your directive. The primary recommendation is to use the **Next.js (React) framework**. This choice offers the best combination of performance for public-facing pages and dynamic interactivity for the private member portal. The UI/UX strategy prioritizes simplicity, accessibility, and a clear distinction between public and private sections to ensure an intuitive experience for all users. The plan includes a robust, responsive contact form built for ease of use across all devices.\\\\n\\\\n**2. Introduction**\\\\n\\\\nThe objective of this analysis is to define a comprehensive frontend strategy that addresses the unique needs of an HOA website. This includes serving two distinct audiences (the general public and authenticated HOA members), providing straightforward communication channels, and ensuring a professional and user-friendly experience. This report details the recomme